All captures390x844, selected static Fraunces/Manrope, protected wordmark, same Open mist. Baseline captures are actual production. Alternatives are throwaway HTML. No request, permission record, chart calculation, storage, microphone or native behavior occurs. Controls illustrate intended routes; no credit for unimplemented validation, draft retention, consent/persistence, focus or native behavior. Prototype checks only establish loaded fonts, no page errors and no horizontal document overflow. Additional captures show decision scroll positions, omen expansion, birth city step/editor and saved result.
OMEN: baseline displays No omen today / One try a day. Next: tomorrow. A explicitly separates no content, today’s already-used attempt, and next attempt tomorrow in always-visible prose. B shows Omen unavailable as a disclosure; its expansion explains used attempt and next day. C uses a two-day timeline: Today/No omen/Attempt used; Tomorrow/Next attempt. Same orb hero and text scaling. Domain fact: a successful daily loader durably claims the local day before generation; cached null means today’s attempt is used, also after deliberate forgetting. It does not establish a particular network error. A storage exception is a separate error and cannot truthfully be assigned used-attempt status without evidence. No proposal grants a second daily attempt. Evaluate clarity, quiet hierarchy and whole-Home fit, not hypothetical generation success.
CONSENT: baseline shows full purpose/data disclosure, explicit switch, and Return to question or Not now, followed by no-send reassurance. Actual baseline verifies retained draft, heading/return focus, explicit permission withdrawal and no request until separate Ask. A uses full disclosure then an explicit Allow and return command, with Not now; no checkbox and no submission. Grant-and-return would require actual explicit consent handling and a withdrawal route in production. B scrolls full disclosure above a persistent switch/return decision area; returning unchecked means no permission, never submit. C presents a data receipt with labeled inputs/saved context/friend data/purposes, then switch, no-send note and return. All permission copy is conditional on granting permission. None implements real permission or request behavior. Judge whether controls and disclosures communicate the same processing boundary, tradeoffs in steps/space and whether decision context remains understandable at200%.
BIRTH: baseline keeps global You header and tabs while editing and viewing saved details. A uses a focused full-screen form, time and city together, Back to You, no global tabs until Done; saved result puts Done before details. B separates time and city into two steps with back; city lookup/selection and calculation are only illustrative. It trades lower per-screen load for more navigation and draft management. C retains You/tab context, presents an editable time/city summary and opens one field at a time; saved details are disclosed after a visible Done. Initial captures have unset values; saved captures all show the same fictional Capricorn Moon/Libra rising/Copenhagen14:30 result. This is not an astronomical calculation or actual storage proof. Real implementation must retain cancellation, search errors, invalid time, original birthday, privacy, draft and focus boundaries.
